# AGENTS.md

Instructions for AI coding agents (and humans) working in SampleApp. SampleApp uses the VSA framework: Vertical Slice Architecture with one folder for each module.

## Layout

| Path             | Purpose                                                              |
|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `SampleApp.csproj` | The one root project. It compiles `App/`, `Features/` and `Plugins/` |
| `App/Host/`      | The web host. `Program.cs` calls `SampleAppApp.Run(args)`. The app class `SampleAppApp` starts the app, finds the modules and the views, and gives the app rules |
| `App/Views/`     | The Blazor views. Each module has its page in `App/Views/Pages/`     |
| `Features/<Name>/`| One feature module: its root class, `Domain/`, `Infrastructure/`, `Presenters/` and `Slices/<Slice>/` |
| `Plugins/<Name>/`| One plugin module: a base of the same layers in each module. It has one folder for each layer, and no `Slices/` |
| `Tests/`         | A nested project: the VSA architecture tests, and one unit test file for each module |

The app has one root project. A module, a slice or a plugin needs no project: add only its files. A nested project (for example `Tests/`) is optional, and the root project does not compile its folder. The folders select the layers, and the rule engine checks each use against the VSA definition after the build.

A command goes from one module to another module. It has one handler and one result. A module sends it with `ICommandSender`. The command is `<Name>Command`, its result `<Name>Result` and its handler `<Name>Handler`, in `Slices/<Name>/`.

`Domain/Shared/` is the published domain of a module. Other modules read its types, for example the keys in a command. A type there is immutable and holds data only, with no methods (`VSA1005` to `VSA1007`). The rest of `Domain/` stays private to the module. A published domain uses only its own module. The messages, handlers and presenters of a module can use the published domain of all modules.

A plugin module is for base types of the app, as the VSA framework is the base of each layer: for example a command base in `Plugins/Game/Contracts/Commands/GameStep.cs`. A plugin module has the layout of the framework: one folder for each layer, for example `Contracts/Commands/` and `Handlers/Commands/`, and no `Slices/`. Each layer of each module can use the same layer of each plugin module. A plugin module never uses a module. Its root has one class that inherits `Plugin` (`VSA1014`). The host configures the plugin modules first, by name, and it loads them through the modules.

## Commands

Use the `vsa` tool to add code. It writes only new files, and it does not write over a file.

```bash
dotnet tool restore                          # one time, after a new clone
dotnet vsa add module <Name>                 # the module root, a status request and a page
dotnet vsa add request <Name> -m <Module>    # a request, its response and its handler
dotnet vsa add command <Name> -m <Module>    # a command, its result and its handler
dotnet vsa add plugin <Name>                 # a plugin module with a base command
dotnet vsa doctor                            # the tool, VSA.Sdk and the packages have one version
dotnet test SampleApp.slnx                   # the architecture tests and the unit tests
```

The build adds the architecture tests to `Tests/`. They check each type against the VSA definition: `VSA2001` (a use that the definition denies), `VSA2101` (a type that is not the type of its part) and `VSA2102` (a type in a folder that the definition does not have). To change the rules, override `Rules(Map)` in `App/Host/SampleAppApp.cs`. To add a test of the app, write `public partial class ArchitectureTests` in `Tests/`.

The VSA packages come from GitHub Packages. Set `GITHUB_TOKEN` before a restore, for example to the output of `gh auth token`.

To update VSA, change the version in `global.json`, `dotnet-tools.json` and the `VSA.*` package references, then run `dotnet vsa doctor`.

## Core principles

- **Organize by slice, not by layer.** Each slice is one folder, `Slices/<Slice>/`, with its request, response, events and handler. A slice has one kind of handler.
- **Modules are independent.** A module uses another module only through its contracts and its published domain.
- **Keep shared code small.** Share code only when two or more slices need it.
